BOJ 1199 — Euler CircuitModel the input as a multigraphThe adjacency matrix gives the number of undirected edges between each pair of vertices. Parallel edges are distinct and must each appear in the circuit. A diagonal entry represents loops; each loop contributes two to its vertex’s degree but is traversed once.An undirected graph has an Euler circuit only if every vertex has even degree and all vertices incident to an edge belong to one connected component. Isolated vertices do not prevent a circuit. The source solution checked only degree parity, so it could print a partial circuit for two disconnected even-degree components.Iterative Hierholzer traversalStart at any vertex with an incident edge. Repeatedly consume one remaining edge and push its other endpoint onto a stack. When the top vertex has no unused edges, append it to the circuit and backtrack. Reversing that list gives an Euler circuit if every edge was reached.The implementation stores multiplicities in a flat matrix. It does not recurse, and it verifies connectivity implicitly by checking that the resulting circuit contains exactly $E+1$ vertices. This ignores isolated vertices while rejecting disconnected components that still contain edges.If the graph has no edges, the zero-edge circuit is reported as vertex 1.C++17#include &amp;lt;algorithm&amp;gt;#include &amp;lt;cstddef&amp;gt;#include &amp;lt;iostream&amp;gt;#include &amp;lt;vector&amp;gt;using namespace std;int main() { ios::sync_with_stdio(false); cin.tie(nullptr); int n; cin &amp;gt;&amp;gt; n; vector&amp;lt;int&amp;gt; remaining(static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(n) * n, 0); vector&amp;lt;long long&amp;gt; degree(n, 0); long long edge_count = 0; for (int u = 0; u &amp;lt; n; ++u) { for (int v = 0; v &amp;lt; n; ++v) { int count; cin &amp;gt;&amp;gt; count; if (u &amp;gt; v || count == 0) continue; remaining[static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(u) * n + v] = count; edge_count += count; if (u == v) { degree[u] += 2LL * count; } else { remaining[static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(v) * n + u] = count; degree[u] += count; degree[v] += count; } } } for (long long value : degree) { if (value % 2 != 0) { cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; -1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; return 0; } } int start = -1; for (int u = 0; u &amp;lt; n; ++u) { if (degree[u] &amp;gt; 0) { start = u; break; } } if (start == -1) { cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; return 0; } vector&amp;lt;int&amp;gt; next_neighbor(n, 0); vector&amp;lt;int&amp;gt; stack; vector&amp;lt;int&amp;gt; circuit; stack.reserve(static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(edge_count) + 1); circuit.reserve(static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(edge_count) + 1); stack.push_back(start); while (!stack.empty()) { int u = stack.back(); int&amp;amp; v = next_neighbor[u]; while (v &amp;lt; n &amp;amp;&amp;amp; remaining[static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(u) * n + v] == 0) { ++v; } if (v == n) { circuit.push_back(u); stack.pop_back(); } else { int w = v; --remaining[static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(u) * n + w]; if (u != w) { --remaining[static_cast&amp;lt;size_t&amp;gt;(w) * n + u]; } stack.push_back(w); } } if (static_cast&amp;lt;long long&amp;gt;(circuit.size()) != edge_count + 1) { cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; -1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; return 0; } reverse(circuit.begin(), circuit.end()); for (size_t i = 0; i &amp;lt; circuit.size(); ++i) { if (i &amp;gt; 0) cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39; &#39;; cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; circuit[i] + 1; } cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;;}The running time is $O(N^2+E)$: the matrix is scanned once, each edge copy is consumed once, and each row pointer advances at most $N$ times. The matrix uses $O(N^2)$ space; the traversal stack and circuit use $O(E)$ space.Source historyAdapted from “백준 1199번 - 오일러 회로”, originally published on 2022-04-26 by MINJUN PARK and marked CC BY 4.0. This version preserves the multigraph/Hierholzer approach, adds the missing connectivity condition, and replaces recursive traversal with an iterative implementation.References BOJ 1199 — Euler Circuit Eulerian path and circuit

LinkKey observationFind the longest substring of S that is not a palindrome. If the whole string is not a palindrome, the answer is N. If it is a palindrome and all characters are identical, every substring is a palindrome, so the answer is -1. Otherwise, the answer is N - 1.ProofIf the whole string is not a palindrome, S itself is a non-palindromic substring of length N, so the answer is N. If the whole string is a palindrome and all characters are identical, every substring consists of identical characters and is therefore a palindrome; the answer is -1.Now suppose S is a palindrome but its characters are not all identical. The only substring of length N is S, so no non-palindromic substring can be longer than N. The only substrings of length N - 1 are obtained by removing the first or last character. Since S is a palindrome, these two strings are reversals of each other, so if one is a palindrome, the other is too.Suppose both were palindromes. In particular, the prefix obtained by removing the last character would be a palindrome. Symmetry of the full string gives S[i] = S[N - 1 - i], while symmetry of that prefix gives S[i] = S[N - 2 - i]. Thus, for i = 0, …, N - 2, S[N - 1 - i] = S[N - 2 - i]. These equalities cover every adjacent pair, forcing all characters to be identical, a contradiction. Therefore both length-N - 1 substrings are non-palindromes, and the answer is N - 1.One scan checks whether the string is a palindrome and whether all its characters are identical. The time complexity is O(N), and the extra space complexity beyond the input string is O(1).C++17 implementation#include &amp;lt;iostream&amp;gt;#include &amp;lt;string&amp;gt;using namespace std;int main() { ios::sync_with_stdio(false); cin.tie(nullptr); string s; cin &amp;gt;&amp;gt; s; const int n = static_cast&amp;lt;int&amp;gt;(s.size()); bool isPalindrome = true; bool allSame = true; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; n; ++i) { if (s[i] != s[n - 1 - i]) isPalindrome = false; if (s[i] != s[0]) allSame = false; } if (!isPalindrome) cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; n &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; else if (allSame) cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; -1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; else cout &amp;lt;&amp;lt; n - 1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; &#39;
&#39;; return 0;}

Problem: BOJ 3977 — Soccer Tactics · 한국어 · 日本語The graph is divided into strongly connected components (SCCs). In the condensation graph, every SCC is a vertex and every edge between distinct SCCs is directed. The components with indegree zero are exactly the groups that can be reached from no other group. If there is exactly one such source component, all its vertices are the required starting players; if there is more than one, the answer is Confused.Kosaraju’s algorithm finds the SCCs in two iterative passes. The first pass performs depth-first search on the original graph and records vertices when their exploration finishes. An explicit vertex stack and a per-vertex next-edge index reproduce recursive DFS exit order without using the call stack. The second pass visits vertices in reverse finishing order on the reversed graph; each traversal identifies one SCC.While reading the edges, the solution stores both graph directions and keeps the original edge list. After assigning SCC IDs, it marks the destination component of every cross-component edge as having incoming edges. It then counts source components and, when there is exactly one, scans vertices in increasing order to print that SCC. This also handles isolated vertices and a graph with no edges: each isolated vertex is its own source SCC.The algorithm takes O(V + E) time and O(V + E) memory. It uses no recursion, so a chain of 100,000 vertices cannot overflow the call stack. A blank line is emitted between test cases.Javaimport java.io.BufferedInputStream;import java.io.IOException;import java.util.ArrayList;public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception { FastScanner input = new FastScanner(); int testCases = input.nextInt(); StringBuilder answer = new StringBuilder(); for (int test = 0; test &amp;lt; testCases; test++) { int n = input.nextInt(); int m = input.nextInt(); ArrayList&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt;[] graph = new ArrayList[n]; ArrayList&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt;[] reverse = new ArrayList[n]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; n; i++) { graph[i] = new ArrayList&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(); reverse[i] = new ArrayList&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(); } int[] from = new int[m]; int[] to = new int[m]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; m; i++) { int a = input.nextInt(); int b = input.nextInt(); from[i] = a; to[i] = b; graph[a].add(b); reverse[b].add(a); } boolean[] visited = new boolean[n]; int[] nextEdge = new int[n]; int[] stack = new int[n]; int[] order = new int[n]; int orderSize = 0; for (int start = 0; start &amp;lt; n; start++) { if (visited[start]) { continue; } int top = 0; stack[0] = start; visited[start] = true; while (top &amp;gt;= 0) { int node = stack[top]; if (nextEdge[node] &amp;lt; graph[node].size()) { int neighbor = graph[node].get(nextEdge[node]++); if (!visited[neighbor]) { visited[neighbor] = true; stack[++top] = neighbor; } } else { order[orderSize++] = node; top--; } } } int[] component = new int[n]; int componentCount = 0; for (int i = orderSize - 1; i &amp;gt;= 0; i--) { int start = order[i]; if (component[start] != 0) { continue; } int top = 0; stack[0] = start; component[start] = ++componentCount; while (top &amp;gt;= 0) { int node = stack[top--]; for (int neighbor : reverse[node]) { if (component[neighbor] == 0) { component[neighbor] = componentCount; stack[++top] = neighbor; } } } } boolean[] hasIncoming = new boolean[componentCount + 1]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; m; i++) { if (component[from[i]] != component[to[i]]) { hasIncoming[component[to[i]]] = true; } } int source = 0; int sourceCount = 0; for (int id = 1; id &amp;lt;= componentCount; id++) { if (!hasIncoming[id]) { source = id; sourceCount++; } } if (test &amp;gt; 0) { answer.append(&#39;
&#39;); } if (sourceCount != 1) { answer.append(&quot;Confused
&quot;); } else { for (int vertex = 0; vertex &amp;lt; n; vertex++) { if (component[vertex] == source) { answer.append(vertex).append(&#39;
&#39;); } } } } System.out.print(answer); } private static class FastScanner { private final BufferedInputStream input = new BufferedInputStream(System.in); int nextInt() throws IOException { int value = 0; int c; do { c = input.read(); } while (c &amp;lt;= &#39; &#39; &amp;amp;&amp;amp; c != -1); while (c &amp;gt; &#39; &#39;) { value = value * 10 + c - &#39;0&#39;; c = input.read(); } return value; } }}

[Link] https://www.acmicpc.net/problem/3665 · 한국어 · 日本語The previous year’s ranking gives a complete order: for every pair of teams, the higher-ranked team has a directed edge to the lower-ranked team. Each reported change reverses exactly one edge. Updating both the adjacency matrix and the destination’s indegree keeps the graph ready for topological sorting.Kahn’s algorithm repeatedly removes a team whose indegree is zero. If more than one team is available at any step, there are multiple possible next teams, so the final ranking is ambiguous. If fewer than N teams can be removed, the changed comparisons contain a cycle and no ranking exists. A cycle takes precedence over ambiguity: print IMPOSSIBLE if the process does not output every team; otherwise print ? when any step had multiple choices, and print the ordering only when every step was forced.The initial graph has N(N - 1) / 2 edges. Building it and scanning every possible edge during Kahn’s algorithm take O(N²) time; processing the M reversals takes O(M), for O(N² + M) total time and O(N²) space.import java.io.BufferedInputStream;import java.io.IOException;import java.util.ArrayDeque;public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException { FastScanner input = new FastScanner(); int testCases = input.nextInt(); StringBuilder answer = new StringBuilder(); while (testCases-- &amp;gt; 0) { int n = input.nextInt(); int[] previous = new int[n]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; n; i++) { previous[i] = input.nextInt() - 1; } boolean[][] edge = new boolean[n][n]; int[] indegree = new int[n]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; n; i++) { for (int j = i + 1; j &amp;lt; n; j++) { edge[previous[i]][previous[j]] = true; indegree[previous[j]]++; } } int changes = input.nextInt(); for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; changes; i++) { int a = input.nextInt() - 1; int b = input.nextInt() - 1; if (edge[a][b]) { edge[a][b] = false; edge[b][a] = true; indegree[b]--; indegree[a]++; } else { edge[b][a] = false; edge[a][b] = true; indegree[a]--; indegree[b]++; } } ArrayDeque&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt; queue = new ArrayDeque&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(); for (int team = 0; team &amp;lt; n; team++) { if (indegree[team] == 0) { queue.addLast(team); } } int[] ranking = new int[n]; int count = 0; boolean ambiguous = false; while (!queue.isEmpty()) { if (queue.size() &amp;gt; 1) { ambiguous = true; } int team = queue.removeFirst(); ranking[count++] = team; for (int next = 0; next &amp;lt; n; next++) { if (edge[team][next] &amp;amp;&amp;amp; --indegree[next] == 0) { queue.addLast(next); } } } if (count &amp;lt; n) { answer.append(&quot;IMPOSSIBLE
&quot;); } else if (ambiguous) { answer.append(&quot;?
&quot;); } else { for (int team : ranking) { answer.append(team + 1).append(&#39; &#39;); } answer.setLength(answer.length() - 1); answer.append(&#39;
&#39;); } } System.out.print(answer); } private static class FastScanner { private final BufferedInputStream input = new BufferedInputStream(System.in); int nextInt() throws IOException { int value = 0; int c; do { c = input.read(); } while (c &amp;lt;= &#39; &#39; &amp;amp;&amp;amp; c != -1); while (c &amp;gt; &#39; &#39;) { value = value * 10 + c - &#39;0&#39;; c = input.read(); } return value; } }}

ProblemDynamic programming on the two cars’ most recent incidentsLet dp[a][b] be the minimum distance needed to handle all remaining incidents when car 1 most recently handled incident a and car 2 most recently handled incident b. Index 0 means the car has not handled an incident: car 1 starts at (1, 1) and car 2 at (N, N). A positive index refers to that incident’s coordinates.Since the cars together have handled every incident through max(a, b), the next one is max(a, b) + 1. There are only two choices: assign it to car 1 or car 2. The transition adds the Manhattan distance from that car’s current position to the next incident:dp[a][b] = 0 if max(a, b) = Wnext = max(a, b) + 1dp[a][b] = min( distance(car 1 position(a), incident[next]) + dp[next][b], distance(car 2 position(b), incident[next]) + dp[a][next])Each transition handles one more incident, so recursion reaches the terminal state; choosing the smaller of the two options preserves the minimum-cost invariant. Memoization computes each (a, b) state at most once. After computing the optimum, compare the same two costs at each step to reconstruct the assignment. Either choice is optimal on a tie; this implementation chooses car 1. When W = 0, the initial state is already terminal, so it prints cost 0 and no assignment lines. Time and space are both O(W²).import java.io.BufferedInputStream;import java.io.IOException;public class Main { private static int n; private static int w; private static int[][] incidents; private static int[][] memo; public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException { FastScanner input = new FastScanner(); n = input.nextInt(); w = input.nextInt(); incidents = new int[w + 1][2]; for (int i = 1; i &amp;lt;= w; i++) { incidents[i][0] = input.nextInt(); incidents[i][1] = input.nextInt(); } memo = new int[w + 1][w + 1]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt;= w; i++) { for (int j = 0; j &amp;lt;= w; j++) { memo[i][j] = -1; } } StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder(); output.append(minimumDistance(0, 0)).append(&#39;
&#39;); int car1Last = 0; int car2Last = 0; while (Math.max(car1Last, car2Last) &amp;lt; w) { int next = Math.max(car1Last, car2Last) + 1; int car1Cost = distance(1, car1Last, next) + minimumDistance(next, car2Last); int car2Cost = distance(2, car2Last, next) + minimumDistance(car1Last, next); if (car1Cost &amp;lt;= car2Cost) { output.append(1).append(&#39;
&#39;); car1Last = next; } else { output.append(2).append(&#39;
&#39;); car2Last = next; } } System.out.print(output); } private static int minimumDistance(int car1Last, int car2Last) { if (Math.max(car1Last, car2Last) == w) return 0; if (memo[car1Last][car2Last] != -1) return memo[car1Last][car2Last]; int next = Math.max(car1Last, car2Last) + 1; int assignToCar1 = distance(1, car1Last, next) + minimumDistance(next, car2Last); int assignToCar2 = distance(2, car2Last, next) + minimumDistance(car1Last, next); memo[car1Last][car2Last] = Math.min(assignToCar1, assignToCar2); return memo[car1Last][car2Last]; } private static int distance(int car, int lastIncident, int nextIncident) { int startRow = lastIncident == 0 ? (car == 1 ? 1 : n) : incidents[lastIncident][0]; int startColumn = lastIncident == 0 ? (car == 1 ? 1 : n) : incidents[lastIncident][1]; return Math.abs(startRow - incidents[nextIncident][0]) + Math.abs(startColumn - incidents[nextIncident][1]); } private static final class FastScanner { private final BufferedInputStream input = new BufferedInputStream(System.in); private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 16]; private int length; private int position; private int read() throws IOException { if (position == length) { length = input.read(buffer); position = 0; if (length == -1) return -1; } return buffer[position++]; } int nextInt() throws IOException { int c; do { c = read(); } while (c &amp;lt;= &#39; &#39; &amp;amp;&amp;amp; c != -1); int value = 0; while (c &amp;gt; &#39; &#39;) { value = value * 10 + c - &#39;0&#39;; c = read(); } return value; } }}

Problem: BOJ 11780 — Floyd 2Given a directed graph with nonnegative edge costs, find the minimum cost between every ordered pair of cities and print one minimum-cost route for each reachable pair. Multiple edges may connect the same ordered pair; only their cheapest cost can matter. The diagonal starts at zero, representing the empty route from a city to itself.Floyd–Warshall with a next-hop matrixdistance[i][j] stores the best known cost from i to j. next[i][j] stores the first city after i on that route, or -1 when no route is known. For a direct edge i -&amp;gt; j, that first city is j.When considering city k as an intermediate, a route through k costs distance[i][k] + distance[k][j]. If this is strictly cheaper than the current route, update both the cost and its first hop: the route from i to k begins with next[i][k], so the newly improved route from i to j has the same first hop. Strict improvement avoids replacing an existing route on ties. Because edge costs are nonnegative and the diagonal is zero, a shortest route can be chosen without cycles; following the first-hop entries therefore reaches the destination. Self-routes are not printed as paths, as required by the problem.Use a finite long sentinel and skip a relaxation if either half-route is unreachable. This prevents adding the sentinel to a finite value. The cost matrix takes O(N^3) time to compute and O(N^2) space; printing all routes costs O(N^2 + total path output), or O(N^2) plus the number of emitted city labels.The output consists of the cost matrix first, using 0 for unreachable pairs, then one route record for every ordered pair in row-major order. An unreachable pair and a pair with identical endpoints each have a single 0 line. Every other reachable pair has its number of cities followed by the city sequence, including both endpoints.import java.io.BufferedInputStream;import java.io.IOException;public class Main { private static final long INF = Long.MAX_VALUE / 4; public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException { FastScanner input = new FastScanner(); int cityCount = input.nextInt(); int routeCount = input.nextInt(); long[][] distance = new long[cityCount][cityCount]; int[][] next = new int[cityCount][cityCount]; for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; cityCount; i++) { java.util.Arrays.fill(distance[i], INF); java.util.Arrays.fill(next[i], -1); distance[i][i] = 0; } for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; routeCount; i++) { int from = input.nextInt() - 1; int to = input.nextInt() - 1; long cost = input.nextLong(); if (cost &amp;lt; distance[from][to]) { distance[from][to] = cost; next[from][to] = to; } } for (int middle = 0; middle &amp;lt; cityCount; middle++) { for (int from = 0; from &amp;lt; cityCount; from++) { if (distance[from][middle] == INF) { continue; } for (int to = 0; to &amp;lt; cityCount; to++) { if (distance[middle][to] == INF) { continue; } long candidate = distance[from][middle] + distance[middle][to]; if (candidate &amp;lt; distance[from][to]) { distance[from][to] = candidate; next[from][to] = next[from][middle]; } } } } StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder(); for (int from = 0; from &amp;lt; cityCount; from++) { for (int to = 0; to &amp;lt; cityCount; to++) { if (to &amp;gt; 0) { output.append(&#39; &#39;); } output.append(distance[from][to] == INF ? 0 : distance[from][to]); } output.append(&#39;
&#39;); } int[] path = new int[cityCount + 1]; for (int from = 0; from &amp;lt; cityCount; from++) { for (int to = 0; to &amp;lt; cityCount; to++) { if (from == to || next[from][to] == -1) { output.append(&quot;0
&quot;); continue; } int length = 0; int current = from; path[length++] = current; while (current != to) { current = next[current][to]; path[length++] = current; } output.append(length); for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; length; i++) { output.append(&#39; &#39;).append(path[i] + 1); } output.append(&#39;
&#39;); } } System.out.print(output); } private static class FastScanner { private final BufferedInputStream input = new BufferedInputStream(System.in); private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 &amp;lt;&amp;lt; 16]; private int position; private int length; private int read() throws IOException { if (position == length) { length = input.read(buffer); position = 0; if (length == -1) { return -1; } } return buffer[position++]; } private long nextLong() throws IOException { int c; do { c = read(); } while (c &amp;lt;= &#39; &#39; &amp;amp;&amp;amp; c != -1); long value = 0; while (c &amp;gt; &#39; &#39;) { value = value * 10 + c - &#39;0&#39;; c = read(); } return value; } private int nextInt() throws IOException { return (int) nextLong(); } }}

Problem: BOJ 11779 — Minimum Cost (2)Given a directed graph with nonnegative edge costs, find the minimum cost from the specified source to destination and print one corresponding route. Keep every directed edge in an adjacency list; parallel edges are valid and need no special handling.Dijkstra’s algorithmMaintain distance[v], the smallest source-to-v cost discovered so far, and parent[v], the predecessor used when that distance was last improved. The priority queue holds candidate (vertex, distance) states. Each time the smallest state is removed, discard it if its distance no longer equals distance[vertex]; it was superseded by a better route. Otherwise, relax each outgoing edge and enqueue a new state whenever it improves a neighbor’s distance.The invariant is that each stored distance is the cost of an actual discovered route, and every queued state represents a candidate route. Because all edge costs are nonnegative, when a state with the globally smallest current distance is removed, no later route through an unprocessed vertex can make it smaller. Relaxation therefore eventually finds the minimum distance to every reachable vertex. Whenever a distance improves, its parent records the preceding vertex on that improving route. Following parents backward from the destination to the source reconstructs a minimum-cost route; reversing that sequence gives the required order. If source and destination are the same, the route naturally contains that single vertex.Distances and edge costs use long, and the priority queue compares with Long.compare rather than subtraction, avoiding overflow-prone arithmetic in comparisons and distance accumulation. The output is exactly three lines: minimum cost, number of route vertices, and the route itself.With a binary heap and lazy removal, the time complexity is O((V + E) log(E + 1)) and the space complexity is O(V + E). For a simple graph this is commonly written as O((V + E) log(V + 1)); retaining parallel edges gives the more general bound above.Javaimport java.io.BufferedInputStream;import java.io.IOException;import java.util.ArrayList;import java.util.Arrays;import java.util.List;import java.util.PriorityQueue;public class Main { private static final long INF = Long.MAX_VALUE / 4; private static class Edge { final int to; final long cost; Edge(int to, long cost) { this.to = to; this.cost = cost; } } private static class State { final int vertex; final long distance; State(int vertex, long distance) { this.vertex = vertex; this.distance = distance; } } public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException { FastScanner input = new FastScanner(); int vertexCount = input.nextInt(); int edgeCount = input.nextInt(); List&amp;lt;List&amp;lt;Edge&amp;gt;&amp;gt; graph = new ArrayList&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(vertexCount); for (int vertex = 0; vertex &amp;lt; vertexCount; vertex++) { graph.add(new ArrayList&amp;lt;&amp;gt;()); } for (int i = 0; i &amp;lt; edgeCount; i++) { int from = input.nextInt() - 1; int to = input.nextInt() - 1; long cost = input.nextLong(); graph.get(from).add(new Edge(to, cost)); } int source = input.nextInt() - 1; int destination = input.nextInt() - 1; long[] distance = new long[vertexCount]; int[] parent = new int[vertexCount]; Arrays.fill(distance, INF); Arrays.fill(parent, -1); distance[source] = 0; PriorityQueue&amp;lt;State&amp;gt; queue = new PriorityQueue&amp;lt;&amp;gt;( (left, right) -&amp;gt; Long.compare(left.distance, right.distance)); queue.add(new State(source, 0)); while (!queue.isEmpty()) { State current = queue.poll(); if (current.distance != distance[current.vertex]) { continue; } for (Edge edge : graph.get(current.vertex)) { long candidate = current.distance + edge.cost; if (candidate &amp;lt; distance[edge.to]) { distance[edge.to] = candidate; parent[edge.to] = current.vertex; queue.add(new State(edge.to, candidate)); } } } List&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt; path = new ArrayList&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(); for (int vertex = destination; vertex != -1; vertex = parent[vertex]) { path.add(vertex); if (vertex == source) { break; } } java.util.Collections.reverse(path); StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder(); output.append(distance[destination]).append(&#39;
